(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Contrast Pointers to Constants vs Constant Pointers vs Constant Pointers to Constants with syntax and code examples.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   const Pointers: The Three Placement Variations:) Tj
T*
(   The placement of the 'const' keyword relative to the asterisk \(*\) determines whether the) Tj
T*
(   pointed-to data, the pointer itself, or both are immutable:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   1. Pointer to Constant Data \(const Type *ptr or Type const *ptr\):) Tj
T*
(   - Rule: The memory data pointed to is read-only. The pointer variable itself can be changed to) Tj
T*
(   point to another address.) Tj
T*
(   - Memory Mutability: *ptr = 20; -> COMPILATION ERROR!) Tj
T*
(   - Pointer Mutability: ptr = &other; -> LEGAL!) Tj
T*
(   - Idiom: Used for read-only function parameters: void print_str\(const char *s\);) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Constant Pointer to Mutable Data \(Type * const ptr\):) Tj
T*
(   - Rule: The pointer variable itself is immutable \(locked to a specific address\). The data at) Tj
T*
(   that address can be freely modified.) Tj
T*
(   - Pointer Mutability: ptr = &other; -> COMPILATION ERROR!) Tj
T*
(   - Memory Mutability: *ptr = 20; -> LEGAL!) Tj
T*
(   - Mandatory Requirement: Must be initialized immediately upon declaration!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Constant Pointer to Constant Data \(const Type * const ptr\):) Tj
T*
(   - Rule: Neither the pointer address nor the pointed-to data can be modified.) Tj
T*
(   - Memory Mutability: *ptr = 20; -> COMPILATION ERROR!) Tj
T*
(   - Pointer Mutability: ptr = &other; -> COMPILATION ERROR!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Demonstrative ANSI C Program:) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
ET

