(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   String Tokenization Mechanics: strtok\(\) vs strtok_r\(\):) Tj
T*
(   1. How strtok\(\) Operates:) Tj
T*
(   - Prototype: char *strtok\(char *str, const char *delim\);) Tj
T*
(   - Operation:) Tj
T*
(   a. On the first call, passes the source string 'str'.) Tj
T*
(   b. Scans for the first character not in 'delim' \(token start\).) Tj
T*
(   c. Scans for the next delimiter character and OVERWRITES IT WITH A NULL TERMINATOR \('\\0'\)!) Tj
T*
(   d. Saves its position internally in a hidden static pointer.) Tj
T*
(   e. Returns pointer to token start.) Tj
T*
(   - Subsequent Calls: Caller passes NULL as first argument \(strtok\(NULL, delim\)\) to continue) Tj
T*
(   tokenizing from the saved static position.) Tj
T*
(   - Destructive Mutation: strtok\(\) alters the original string in-place by writing '\\0' bytes!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Reentrancy Hazard:) Tj
T*
(   Because the state pointer is stored in an internal static variable:) Tj
T*
(   - strtok\(\) CANNOT be used concurrently across multiple threads.) Tj
T*
(   - strtok\(\) CANNOT be nested \(e.g. tokenizing an outer line by tabs and inner fields by commas) Tj
T*
(   resets the static pointer!\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Safe Alternative: strtok_r\(\) \(POSIX Reentrant\):) Tj
T*
(   - Prototype: char *strtok_r\(char *str, const char *delim, char **saveptr\);) Tj
T*
(   - Caller explicitly supplies the context pointer 'saveptr', maintaining state on caller's) Tj
T*
(   stack!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Demonstrative ANSI C Program:) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
(   #include <string.h>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int main\(void\) {) Tj
T*
