(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the difference between memcpy\(\) and memmove\(\), detailing memory overlap handling and buffer safety.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Memory Copying: memcpy\(\) vs memmove\(\):) Tj
T*
(   1. Fundamental Prototypes \(<string.h>\):) Tj
T*
(   void *memcpy\(void *dest, const void *src, size_t n\);) Tj
T*
(   void *memmove\(void *dest, const void *src, size_t n\);) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Overlapping Memory Regions:) Tj
T*
(   - memcpy\(\): Assumes that source and destination memory buffers DO NOT OVERLAP in RAM.) Tj
T*
(   If buffers overlap \(e.g., dest = src + 2\), memcpy\(\) produces undefined behavior! On modern) Tj
T*
(   glibc x86-64, optimized SIMD/AVX instructions copy bytes out-of-order, corrupting data.) Tj
T*
(   - memmove\(\): GUARANTEES CORRECT COPYING EVEN IF SOURCE AND DESTINATION OVERLAP!) Tj
T*
(   Internally checks:) Tj
T*
(   If dest < src: Copies bytes forward \(from index 0 to n-1\).) Tj
T*
(   If dest > src: Copies bytes backward \(from index n-1 down to 0\).) Tj
T*
(   This guarantees no source byte is overwritten before being read!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Code Demonstrating the Overlap Bug:) Tj
T*
(   #include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
(   #include <string.h>) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   int main\(void\) {) Tj
T*
(   char str1[32] = "1234567890";) Tj
T*
(   char str2[32] = "1234567890";) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   /* Overlapping shift: copy 6 bytes from index 2 to index 0 */) Tj
T*
(   /* Safe memmove: correctly shifts "345678" into start */) Tj
T*
(   memmove\(str1, str1 + 2, 6\);) Tj
T*
(   printf\("memmove result: %s\\n", str1\); /* Prints "3456787890" */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   /* In-place buffer duplication */) Tj
T*
ET

