(   [Problem Statement]: Explain deep copy vs shallow copy in C structures containing dynamic pointers, detailing memory aliasing and corruption traps.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Deep Copy vs Shallow Copy in C:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definitions:) Tj
T*
(   - Shallow Copy: Duplicates only the top-level structure bits \(member-by-member copy, as) Tj
T*
(   performed by default struct assignment '='\).) Tj
T*
(   If the struct contains a pointer to heap memory, only the memory address pointer is copied!) Tj
T*
(   - Deep Copy: Allocates brand new heap memory for the destination struct and copies the) Tj
T*
(   underlying data elements.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Memory Aliasing Hazard \(Double-Free Bug\):) Tj
T*
(   typedef struct { char *sequence; int len; } GeneRecord;) Tj
T*
(   GeneRecord g1; g1.sequence = malloc\(100\);) Tj
T*
(   GeneRecord g2 = g1; /* SHALLOW COPY! g1 and g2 point to the SAME heap buffer! */) Tj
T*
(   free\(g1.sequence\);) Tj
T*
(   free\(g2.sequence\); /* CRITICAL BUG: Double-Free Heap Corruption Crash! */) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Deep Copy Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   GeneRecord deep_copy_gene\(const GeneRecord *src\) {) Tj
T*
(   GeneRecord dest;) Tj
T*
(   dest.len = src->len;) Tj
T*
(   dest.sequence = \(char *\)malloc\(\(src->len + 1\) * sizeof\(char\)\);) Tj
T*
(   strcpy\(dest.sequence, src->sequence\); /* Distinct memory clone */) Tj
T*
(   return dest;) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
