(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the restrict type qualifier in C99, detailing compiler pointer aliasing assumptions and optimization opportunities.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   The restrict Type Qualifier in C99:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Semantics:) Tj
T*
(   - Introduced in C99, applied strictly to pointer declarations: int * restrict ptr;) Tj
T*
(   - Contract: Informs the compiler that for the lifetime of the pointer, the object pointed to) Tj
T*
(   will ONLY be accessed through that pointer \(or pointers derived directly from it\).) Tj
T*
(   - No Aliasing: Assures the compiler that no other pointer points to \(aliases\) the same memory) Tj
T*
(   location.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Compiler Optimization Benefits:) Tj
T*
(   Without restrict, in:) Tj
T*
(   void add_vectors\(int *a, int *b, int *c, int n\) {) Tj
T*
(   for \(int i = 0; i < n; i++\) a[i] = b[i] + c[i];) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
(   The compiler must conservatively assume that writing to a[i] might mutate b[i] or c[i] \(if) Tj
T*
(   pointers alias\).) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, it cannot cache b[i] in registers and cannot vectorize using SIMD instructions.) Tj
T*
(   With restrict, the compiler emits unrolled AVX/SSE vector instructions, boosting execution) Tj
T*
(   speed by 4x!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
ET

