(   [Problem Statement]: Explain AddressSanitizer \(ASan\) and Valgrind tools for detecting C pointer corruption, memory leaks, and out-of-bounds errors.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Memory Diagnostics: AddressSanitizer and Valgrind:) Tj
T*
(   1. AddressSanitizer \(ASan\):) Tj
T*
(   - Compiler-instrumentation tool built into GCC and Clang.) Tj
T*
(   - Compilation Flag: gcc -fsanitize=address -g program.c -o program) Tj
T*
(   - How it works: Surrounds all allocations with 'Redzones' and uses 'Shadow Memory' to track) Tj
T*
(   address validity.) Tj
T*
(   - Catches: Out-of-bounds accesses \(heap, stack, global\), Use-After-Free, Double-Free with) Tj
T*
(   near-zero runtime overhead \(~2x slowdown\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Valgrind \(Memcheck\):) Tj
T*
(   - Dynamic binary instrumentation tool \(no recompilation needed\).) Tj
T*
(   - Execution Command: valgrind --leak-check=full --track-origins=yes ./program) Tj
T*
(   - How it works: Emulates CPU and tracks validity of every memory byte in synthetic shadow) Tj
T*
(   registers.) Tj
T*
(   - Catches: Uninitialized variable reads, exact leak line numbers, mismatched frees) Tj
T*
(   \(malloc/delete\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
